  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "One run on four hits and ten strikeouts says everything about why they lost.",
    "what_this_means": "They had their chances and couldn't convert. Twelve plate appearances with runners in scoring position netted just one run. Critical misses in the second and seventh innings when the game was still within reach. Warren pitched well enough to win, giving them 5.1 innings of three runs, but the offense never answered. An inning-by-inning drought defined this: empty at-bats in the second, fifth, seventh, and eighth. St. Louis scored three in the fifth and the Yankees never recovered. This is a team that can't afford to waste pitching performances like this one. When you leave that many runners on base in a two-run game, you're not losing by accident.",
